faqs:
  - q: "How often should smoke alarms be tested?"
    a: "Press the test button monthly — it takes ten seconds while the kettle boils. Give each alarm a vacuum around the vents a couple of times a year, because dust and the odd spider are behind most false alarms and slow sensors. Then have the set properly checked (dates, placement, sensor condition) every year or so, which is the visit this page is about."
  - q: "What does your maintenance visit include that I can't do myself?"
    a: "The button test you can do — and should. What we add: reading the manufacture dates and building you a replacement timeline, checking each alarm is the right type and in a spot that meets current guidance, cleaning sensor vents properly, testing interconnection actually works from every alarm (people are regularly surprised), and writing it all down. The report matters more than people expect — for insurance, for rentals, and for simply not having to think about it again for a year."
  - q: "Is the vacuum cleaner thing real, or electrician folklore?"
    a: "Completely real. Photoelectric alarms work by detecting particles that scatter light — and dust particles scatter light just fine. A dusty chamber causes random false alarms (very popular at 2am) or dulls the sensor's response. A gentle vacuum around the vents twice a year keeps the chamber clean. It's the least glamorous fire safety advice we give, and some of the most effective."
  - q: "Do you do testing for businesses too?"
    a: "Yes — small commercial premises like offices, shops and cafes with domestic-style alarms are straightforward for us. Larger buildings with monitored fire panel systems need a fire protection company for their scheduled inspections, and we'll tell you honestly if that's your setup. If you're not sure which world your building lives in, send us a photo and we'll point you the right way."
